1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,785
Imagine you start generating a report,

2
00:00:02,805 --> 00:00:05,215
but close the browser before it finishes.

3
00:00:05,235 --> 00:00:07,725
You might assume the server stops working too.

4
00:00:07,745 --> 00:00:09,880
But often, it doesn't.

5
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:14,675
The client connection is gone.

6
00:00:14,695 --> 00:00:19,100
But the application may still be executing code, holding a database connection,

7
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:22,860
or running an expensive query that nobody needs anymore.

8
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,885
That's where cancellation comes in.

9
00:00:29,905 --> 00:00:32,810
Instead of assuming work stops automatically,

10
00:00:32,830 --> 00:00:35,008
the application needs a way to communicate

11
00:00:35,028 --> 00:00:37,860
that the result is no longer required.

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,620
When the client disconnects, the server can detect that signal.

13
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:46,200
It can pass cancellation through the service layer,

14
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:48,590
and into supported database operations,

15
00:00:48,610 --> 00:00:51,060
allowing unnecessary work to stop.

16
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,225
Cancellation is cooperative.

17
00:00:55,245 --> 00:00:58,015
Your code checks whether the operation was cancelled,

18
00:00:58,035 --> 00:01:01,475
and supported asynchronous operations receive the same signal.

19
00:01:01,495 --> 00:01:05,340
It isn't a magic switch that instantly kills every task.

20
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:10,585
But there's a catch. If your code never checks for cancellation,

21
00:01:10,605 --> 00:01:12,980
or calls a library that doesn't support it,

22
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,915
the work may continue anyway.

23
00:01:14,935 --> 00:01:18,620
That's why cancellation must be handled deliberately.

24
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,660
A timeout is related, but different.

25
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:26,680
Cancellation may happen because the client leaves.

26
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:31,035
A timeout happens because a deadline is reached. Both are useful, but neither

27
00:01:31,055 --> 00:01:34,580
guarantees an operation stops immediately.

28
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:40,025
The correct design propagates cancellation through the request,

29
00:01:40,045 --> 00:01:41,990
checks it during long-running work,

30
00:01:42,010 --> 00:01:45,220
and releases resources when operations end.

31
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,275
Remember: a disconnected client

32
00:01:49,295 --> 00:01:51,760
doesn't always mean cancelled work.

33
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:55,820
Make cancellation an explicit part of your backend design.
